<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
<meta charset="utf-8">
<title>{{REPORT_TITLE}}</title>
<!--
  CONTRACT for scripts/build-report.mjs (owned by another workstream).
  This file is the 5-PAGE "Current Month" report layout:
    Page 1: Executive Summary (KPI row + 2×2 findings grid)
    Page 2: Traffic (daily stacked-bar chart + device compare table)
    Page 3: Core Web Vitals (3 metric-tile summaries + 3 distribution histograms + 3 daily trend charts)
    Page 4: Top Pages (page performance scorecard table)
    Page 5: Errors (JS/request rate charts + error count chart + exception/request-error tables)

  Its sibling report-trending.html.tmpl is the 3-page "Trending" layout — pick ONE per render,
  they are two separate template files (not one file with a REPORT_TYPE switch).

  PLACEHOLDERS (plain string replace, each appears exactly once):
    {{REPORT_TITLE}}         - <title> text, e.g. "acme-shop — March 2026 Review"
    {{FRONTEND_NAME}}         - shown top-right of every page header
    {{ENVIRONMENT_NAME}}      - shown under frontend name; pass "" to omit (CSS :empty hides it)
    {{DATE_RANGE_LABEL}}      - shown under the page section title on every page, e.g. "March 2026"
    {{GENERATED_AT}}          - footer timestamp on page 1, e.g. "2026-04-01 09:00 UTC"
    {{FINDINGS_TRAFFIC_HTML}} - pre-rendered HTML (glue script markdown -> HTML) for "## Traffic" notes.
    {{FINDINGS_CWV_HTML}}     - same, for "## Core Web Vitals" notes.
    {{FINDINGS_PAGES_HTML}}   - same, for "## Pages" (Top Pages) notes.
    {{FINDINGS_ERRORS_HTML}}  - same, for "## Error" notes.
    {{DATA_JSON}}             - single JS object literal (valid JSON), assigned to `const DATA = {{DATA_JSON}};`
                                below. Schema (all required unless noted; use [] / null for "no data"):

    DATA = {
      kpis: [ { label, value, change, color, lowerIsBetter }, ... ],   // same shape as trending template.
                                                                        // `color` is accepted for schema
                                                                        // stability with the trending deck but
                                                                        // is NOT rendered here — v2 dropped the
                                                                        // per-metric accent bar (see .kpi-tile
                                                                        // note in deck-base.css); the verdict
                                                                        // hue now lives entirely on the delta chip.

      cmDailyDevice: {                              // stacked bar chart, page 2
        labels: ["1", "2", ... "31"],                // day-of-month labels
        desktop: [120, 130, ...], mobile: [80, 95, ...],
      },
      deviceCompareRows: [                           // table, page 2 (one row per device type)
        { deviceType: "Desktop", sessions: 40200, pageLoads: 38900, lcpP75: 2200, inpP75: 180, clsP75: 0.04 },
        { deviceType: "Mobile",  sessions: 31000, pageLoads: 29800, lcpP75: 3100, inpP75: 260, clsP75: 0.09 },
      ],

      cmCwvTier: {                                    // good/NI/poor % breakdown, folded into the page-3
        lcp: { good: 72, ni: 18, poor: 10 },           // metric-tile's mt-tail line (units: % 0-100) instead of
        inp: { good: 81, ni: 12, poor: 7 },            // a standalone 100%-stacked bar — see the page-3 CSS
        cls: { good: 90, ni: 7,  poor: 3 },            // comment for why.
      },
      cmCwvDistribution: {                             // 3 small histograms, 11 buckets each, page 3
        lcp: { buckets: ["0-1s","1-2s",...,"10s+"], counts: [.. 11 numbers ..], p75: 2200 },
        inp: { buckets: [...11 labels...],           counts: [...11...],        p75: 180 },
        cls: { buckets: ["0-0.1",...,"1.0+"],        counts: [...11...],        p75: 0.04 },
      },                                                // p75 also drives the page-3 metric-tile value + marker.
      cmDailyCwv: {                                     // 3 line charts (device-split + threshold lines), page 3
        labels: ["1", "2", ... "31"],
        lcp: { desktop: [...], mobile: [...] },
        inp: { desktop: [...], mobile: [...] },
        cls: { desktop: [...], mobile: [...] },
      },

      topPagesRows: [                                  // table, page 4 (PagePerfRow shape, top 10 by visits)
        { name: "/checkout", count: 9200, lcp: 2100, inp: 150, cls: 0.03, exceptions: 4, requestErrors: 2 },
      ],

      cmErrorRates: {                                   // 2 line charts (percent), page 5
        labels: ["1", "2", ... "31"],
        jsErrorRate: [0.4, 0.6, ...], reqErrorRate: [1.1, 0.9, ...],
      },
      cmErrorCounts: {                                   // stacked bar chart, page 5 — folded to top 5 + "Other"
        labels: ["1", "2", ... "31"],                     // by the template's own script (see foldErrorTypes)
        byType: { "JS Error": [3,5,...], "Timeout": [1,0,...] },   // any number of series, keyed by error type
      },
      topExceptionRows: [                                // table, page 5 (top 5 rendered)
        { count: 120, type: "TypeError", message: "Cannot read properties of undefined", source: "app.bundle.js" },
      ],
      topRequestErrorRows: [                             // table, page 5 (top 5 rendered)
        { count: 80, method: "GET", status: 500, host: "api.acme.com", path: "/v1/cart" },
      ],
    }

  The template's own <script> block builds every Chart.js chart, the KPI row, every table, and the page-3
  metric tiles from DATA on DOMContentLoaded (and again on `beforeprint`, so PDF export re-reads the light
  print tokens rather than keeping the dark screen colours baked into the first paint — see buildEverything).
  Markdown -> HTML conversion for findings is the glue script's job.
-->
<script src="chart.umd.min.js"></script>
<style>
  /* DT Flow, Dynatrace's brand font, served from Dynatrace's own public CDN
     (same URL @dynatrace/strato-components' AppRoot.css loads it from) —
     requires network access at render time; falls back to system fonts if unreachable.
     Registered as "DynatraceFlow", the name deck-base.css's --dt-font-sans stack falls
     through to after "DT Flow" (a locally-installed family this file never declares). */
  @font-face { font-family: DynatraceFlow; font-weight: 400; font-display: swap; src: url(https://dt-cdn.net/fonts/DTFlow-Regular-v1.2.1.woff2) format('woff2'); }
  @font-face { font-family: DynatraceFlow; font-weight: 500; font-display: swap; src: url(https://dt-cdn.net/fonts/DTFlow-Medium-v1.2.1.woff2) format('woff2'); }
  @font-face { font-family: DynatraceFlow; font-weight: 600; font-display: swap; src: url(https://dt-cdn.net/fonts/DTFlow-Semibold-v1.2.1.woff2) format('woff2'); }
  @font-face { font-family: DynatraceFlow; font-weight: 700; font-display: swap; src: url(https://dt-cdn.net/fonts/DTFlow-Heavy-v1.2.1.woff2) format('woff2'); }

  /* ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
     Shared design layer — pasted verbatim from SCRATCH/deck-base.css.
     See that file's own header comment for provenance. Nothing below this
     block until the "Report-specific additions" marker may hold a raw colour;
     every value here and beyond derives from the ten Strato roles in Layer 1.
     ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ */

  /* ── Layer 1: Strato tokens ────────────────────────────────────────────────
     Dynatrace Strato design tokens, extracted from @dynatrace/strato-design-tokens
     (v1.6.0). Development-time extraction only — the package is NOT a dependency
     of this skill. Each value cites the Strato role it came from, verified against
     the package's own generated DARK stylesheet (styles/variables-dark.css).
     The screen surface is a dark theme, so the dark variant of every role is used;
     @media print at the bottom re-declares this same block at its light values.

     Nothing below this block holds a raw colour of its own. Every semantic alias
     and colour-mix derives from these ten roles, which is the whole reason one
     print override can invert the document without touching another rule. */
  :root {
    --dt-bg-base:              #19192c; /* Colors.Background.Base.Default (dark) */
    --dt-bg-container:         #212135; /* Colors.Background.Container.Neutral.Default (dark) */
    --dt-bg-container-subtle:  #19192c; /* Colors.Background.Container.Neutral.Subdued (dark) */
    --dt-text-primary:         #ebecff; /* Colors.Text.Neutral.Default — body text */
    --dt-text-secondary:       #b1b2d2; /* Colors.Text.Neutral.Subdued — secondary text */
    --dt-border-default:       #3b3b52; /* Colors.Border.Neutral.Default (dark) */
    --dt-critical:             #ff999c; /* Colors.Text.Critical.Default (dark) */
    --dt-warning:              #eea83f; /* Colors.Text.Warning.Default (dark) */
    --dt-success:              #6fc3ba; /* Colors.Text.Success.Default (dark) */
    --dt-primary:              #adb0ff; /* Colors.Text.Primary.Default (dark) — brand-accent text role */

    /* Typography.Text.Base.Default.Family / Typography.Code.Base.Default.Family.
       "DT Flow" leads the stack as the family name the @font-face rules above
       declare; "DynatraceFlow" follows as the name a machine with the font
       installed system-wide exposes; Roboto/Helvetica are the real fallbacks,
       which is the normal case outside Dynatrace. */
    --dt-font-sans: "DT Flow", DynatraceFlow, Roboto, Helvetica, sans-serif;
    --dt-font-mono: "Roboto Mono", ui-monospace, monospace;

    /* Borders.Radius.Container.Default */
    --dt-radius: 9px;
    /* Spacings.Size8 / Size16 / Size32 — confirmed 1:1 in px against variables.css. */
    --dt-space-4: 4px;
    --dt-space-8: 8px;
    --dt-space-16: 16px;
    --dt-space-32: 32px;
  }

  /* ── Layer 2: semantic aliases + type ramp ────────────────────────────────
     Derived shades use colour-mix over the tokens — no new hex anywhere below.
     Because the surface ramp runs dark base → lighter container, every mix over
     --dt-bg-base lightens; under the print override the same mixes darken, and
     each was checked to hold its contrast ratio in both directions. */
  :root {
    --bg-page:                 var(--dt-bg-base);
    --bg-surface:              var(--dt-bg-container-subtle);
    --bg-container:            var(--dt-bg-container);
    --bg-container-emphasized: color-mix(in srgb, var(--dt-border-default) 45%, var(--dt-bg-container));
    --bg-container-primary:    color-mix(in srgb, var(--dt-primary) 8%, var(--dt-bg-base));
    --border-default:          var(--dt-border-default);
    --border-subdued:          color-mix(in srgb, var(--dt-border-default) 55%, var(--dt-bg-base));
    --text-default:            var(--dt-text-primary);
    --text-subdued:            var(--dt-text-secondary);
    --good:                    var(--dt-success);
    --warning:                 var(--dt-warning);
    --critical:                var(--dt-critical);

    /* One type ramp for the whole deck, replacing the ad-hoc
       9/9.5/10/10.5/11/12/13/15/16/18/32px sizes the v1 decks accumulated.
       A page is 297mm ≈ 1122px wide at 96dpi, so these px values are close to
       what a reader actually sees on a printed page — the ramp is deliberately
       one step larger than the portrait document's, because a landscape page is
       read at arm's length or projected, not held. */
    --rp-text-micro:   10px;  /* column headers, chip text, footer */
    --rp-text-small:   12px;  /* dense labels, table cells, axis text */
    --rp-text-body:    13px;  /* body / analyst notes */
    --rp-text-lead:    16px;  /* emphasised numbers, panel subject */
    --rp-text-title:   20px;  /* page section heading */
    --rp-text-display: 30px;  /* KPI figures */
  }

  * { box-sizing: border-box; }

  html, body {
    margin: 0; padding: 0;
    background: var(--bg-page);
    color: var(--text-default);
    font-family: var(--dt-font-sans);
    font-size: var(--rp-text-body);
    /* Every figure in this deck is a number in a column or a number beside a
       bar, so proportional digits are never wanted anywhere in it. */
    font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums;
  }

  /* ── Page ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
     A fixed A4-landscape canvas per topic. The deck is authored at print size
     rather than reflowed, so what a reviewer sees in the browser is what lands
     in the PDF — the point of keeping pages at all. `overflow: hidden` is the
     contract that makes that true: content that does not fit is a layout bug to
     fix at build time, not something the page silently grows to swallow.

     The v1 decks painted a blue radial-dot texture over every page. It is gone:
     it read as a screen-app background rather than as a document, it fought the
     chart marks it sat underneath, and it was the one place in the file that
     used a raw brand hue as decoration instead of as data. */
  @page { size: A4 landscape; margin: 0; }
  .page {
    width: 297mm; height: 210mm;
    /* Centres the fixed-width sheet in a wider browser window. In print the page
       box is the sheet, so auto resolves to 0 and this is a no-op. */
    margin-inline: auto;
    padding: 11mm 13mm 9mm;
    background: var(--bg-page);
    color: var(--text-default);
    display: flex; flex-direction: column;
    page-break-after: always;
    position: relative;
    overflow: hidden;
  }
  .page:last-child { page-break-after: auto; }

  /* ── Masthead ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
     Logo, a hairline divider, the team lockup, then the document's own subject
     on the far side. Deliberately quiet — a deliverable looks official because
     the mark is placed with confidence and the rule under it is crisp, not
     because the branding is large.

     v1 centred a 24px-tracked "TRENDING" between the two halves, which made the
     kind of document the loudest text on every page while the application name —
     the thing a reader needs to know — sat small in the corner. The kind now
     rides as an eyebrow over the section name on the left of the rule, and the
     application takes the display line on the right. */
  .hdr {
    display: flex; align-items: flex-end; justify-content: space-between;
    gap: var(--dt-space-16);
    padding-bottom: 10px;
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--border-default);
    flex: 0 0 auto;
  }
  .hdr-left { display: flex; align-items: center; gap: 13px; }
  .dt-logo { width: 132px; height: auto; display: block; flex: none; }
  /* The wordmark group is fill="currentColor" so it picks up the document ink and
     inverts with the print override; the logo mark keeps its fixed brand colours,
     the one place in this file where a hue is not a design token and must not be. */
  .brand-rule { width: 1px; align-self: stretch; min-height: 20px; background: var(--border-default); }
  /* An all-caps lockup, not UI copy — the one place the deck shouts, and it
     shouts quietly: tracked out, small, and in the secondary ink so it sits
     beside the mark rather than competing with it. */
  .brand-team {
    font-size: var(--rp-text-small); font-weight: 700;
    letter-spacing: .16em; text-transform: uppercase;
    color: var(--text-subdued); white-space: nowrap;
  }
  .hdr-right { text-align: right; display: flex; flex-direction: column; gap: 2px; }
  .hdr-right .kind {
    font-size: var(--rp-text-micro); font-weight: 700;
    letter-spacing: .14em; text-transform: uppercase; color: var(--text-subdued);
  }
  .hdr-right .name { font-size: var(--rp-text-lead); font-weight: 600; color: var(--text-default); line-height: 1.1; }
  .hdr-right .meta { font-size: var(--rp-text-small); color: var(--text-subdued); }
  /* Passed as "" when there is no environment to name; :empty then removes the
     row rather than leaving a gap where a value should be. */
  .hdr-right .meta:empty { display: none; }

  /* Section name for the page, sitting between the masthead rule and the first
     panel so it is the thing a reader lands on after the letterhead. */
  .page-title { flex: 0 0 auto; margin: 9px 0 var(--dt-space-16); }
  .page-title h2 {
    margin: 0; font-size: var(--rp-text-title); font-weight: 600;
    letter-spacing: -.01em; color: var(--text-default);
  }
  .page-title .lede { margin: 3px 0 0; font-size: var(--rp-text-small); color: var(--text-subdued); }
  .page-title .lede:empty { display: none; }

  .content { flex: 1; display: flex; flex-direction: column; gap: var(--dt-space-16); min-height: 0; }

  /* ── Footer ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
     A hairline, the lockup, and the page's own position in the deck. The page
     number is the part that earns the row: a printed deck gets separated, and a
     page with no number cannot be put back. */
  .ftr {
    flex: 0 0 auto; display: flex; align-items: baseline; justify-content: space-between;
    gap: var(--dt-space-16); margin-top: 10px; padding-top: 8px;
    border-top: 1px solid var(--border-subdued);
    font-size: var(--rp-text-micro); color: var(--text-subdued); letter-spacing: .05em;
  }
  .ftr .brand-name { color: var(--text-subdued); font-weight: 700; letter-spacing: .12em; text-transform: uppercase; }

  /* ── Panel ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
     One card idiom for everything on a page: chart, table, notes. v1 ran three
     near-identical card classes (.kpi-tile, .exec-card, .chart-card) that each
     drew their own border colour, radius and gradient top-accent. The gradients
     are gone — a fading brand-blue bar on top of every card is chrome that says
     nothing about the card's contents, and with one per card it stopped
     distinguishing anything at all. What distinguishes a panel now is its
     heading and, where the content is graded, its status accent. */
  .panel {
    background: var(--bg-container);
    border: 1px solid var(--border-default);
    border-radius: var(--dt-radius);
    padding: 13px 15px 11px;
    display: flex; flex-direction: column; min-height: 0; min-width: 0;
  }
  .panel-title {
    flex: 0 0 auto; margin: 0 0 9px;
    font-size: var(--rp-text-micro); font-weight: 700;
    text-transform: uppercase; letter-spacing: .1em; color: var(--text-subdued);
  }
  /* A short sentence under a panel title, for the qualifier a title cannot carry
     ("p75 across N loads", "top 10 by visits"). */
  .panel-note { flex: 0 0 auto; margin: -5px 0 9px; font-size: var(--rp-text-small); color: var(--text-subdued); }
  .panel-note strong { color: var(--text-default); font-weight: 600; }
  .panel-row { display: flex; gap: var(--dt-space-16); flex: 1; min-height: 0; }
  .empty-note { color: var(--text-subdued); font-size: var(--rp-text-small); font-style: italic; }

  /* ── KPI tiles ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
     The figure is the tile. Label above it in the subdued ink, delta below in a
     chip whose hue is the verdict — and the verdict is never colour alone: the
     chip carries an arrow glyph and the delta keeps its sign, so the tile reads
     stripped of colour and in print.

     v1 hung a 3px accent bar off each tile's top edge in a per-KPI hue. It is
     dropped: the hue was decorative (assigned per metric, not per state), and it
     collided with the delta chip, which is the one thing in the tile that a hue
     should mean something in. */
  .kpi-row { display: flex; gap: var(--dt-space-8); flex: 0 0 auto; }
  .kpi-tile {
    flex: 1; min-width: 0;
    background: var(--bg-surface);
    border: 1px solid var(--border-default);
    border-radius: var(--dt-radius);
    padding: 11px 14px 10px;
  }
  .kpi-label {
    font-size: var(--rp-text-micro); font-weight: 700; color: var(--text-subdued);
    text-transform: uppercase; letter-spacing: .1em;
    white-space: nowrap; overflow: hidden; text-overflow: ellipsis;
  }
  .kpi-value {
    margin-top: 7px;
    font-size: var(--rp-text-display); font-weight: 700; line-height: 1;
    letter-spacing: -.015em; color: var(--text-default);
  }
  .kpi-unit { font-size: var(--rp-text-small); font-weight: 600; color: var(--text-subdued); margin-left: 5px; letter-spacing: .04em; }
  /* One accent per delta chip, set from the direction+goodness the renderer
     resolved, and consumed by text, fill and border so the three cannot
     disagree. --kpi-accent is set inline by the renderer. */
  .kpi-change {
    display: inline-flex; align-items: center; gap: 4px; margin-top: 9px;
    font-size: var(--rp-text-small); font-weight: 700;
    padding: 2px 7px; border-radius: 999px;
    color: var(--kpi-accent, var(--text-subdued));
    background: color-mix(in srgb, var(--kpi-accent, var(--text-subdued)) 14%, transparent);
    border: 1px solid color-mix(in srgb, var(--kpi-accent, var(--text-subdued)) 45%, transparent);
  }
  .kpi-change.good    { --kpi-accent: var(--good); }
  .kpi-change.bad     { --kpi-accent: var(--critical); }
  .kpi-change.neutral { --kpi-accent: var(--text-subdued); }
  .kpi-change svg { flex: none; display: block; }
  .kpi-sub { display: block; margin-top: 6px; font-size: var(--rp-text-micro); color: var(--text-subdued); }

  /* ── Analyst-note cards ───────────────────────────────────────────────────
     Prose written by a person, edited in place in the HTML before the PDF is
     rendered. Each card is a .panel; the section it belongs to is named by its
     title, not by a coloured top bar in a hue picked per section (v1's
     purple/amber/teal, none of which meant anything — amber in particular read
     as a warning state on a card that might be reporting good news). */
  .notes-body {
    flex: 1; min-height: 0; overflow: hidden;
    font-size: var(--rp-text-body); line-height: 1.55; color: var(--text-default);
  }
  .notes-body ul { margin: 0; padding-left: 17px; }
  .notes-body li { margin-bottom: 6px; }
  .notes-body li:last-child { margin-bottom: 0; }
  .notes-body p { margin: 0 0 7px; }
  .notes-body p:last-child { margin-bottom: 0; }
  .notes-body strong { font-weight: 600; }
  /* --border-default is nowhere near the 3:1 an interactive-component outline
     needs; --dt-primary is the link/emphasis role and clears it, so an editable
     region borrowing it reads as "this is interactive", which is the point. */
  .notes-body[contenteditable="true"]:focus { outline: 1px dashed var(--dt-primary); outline-offset: 4px; }

  /* Mini-stat chips pinned to the bottom of a notes card: the two or three
     figures the prose above refers to, so a reader can check the claim without
     turning the page. */
  .mini-stats {
    flex: 0 0 auto; margin-top: 12px; padding-top: 9px;
    border-top: 1px solid var(--border-subdued);
    display: flex; flex-direction: column; gap: 6px;
  }
  .mini-period { font-size: var(--rp-text-micro); font-weight: 700; text-transform: uppercase; letter-spacing: .1em; color: var(--text-subdued); }
  .mini-chips { display: flex; gap: 6px; align-items: stretch; }
  .mini-chip {
    flex: 1; min-width: 0; text-align: center;
    background: var(--bg-page);
    border: 1px solid var(--border-subdued);
    border-radius: 6px; padding: 5px 7px;
  }
  .mini-chip-label {
    font-size: var(--rp-text-micro); color: var(--text-subdued);
    text-transform: uppercase; letter-spacing: .07em;
    white-space: nowrap; overflow: hidden; text-overflow: ellipsis;
  }
  .mini-chip-value { font-size: var(--rp-text-small); font-weight: 700; color: var(--text-default); line-height: 1.3; }

  /* ── Status chip + rating vocabulary ──────────────────────────────────────
     Strato's status vocabulary: colour is never the only carrier of a verdict.
     Each chip pairs its hue with a shaped icon (circle+check ideal,
     triangle+exclamation warning, diamond+X critical) and the rating spelled
     out, so the chip survives greyscale printing and colour-blind readers. */
  .status {
    display: inline-flex; align-items: center; gap: 5px; flex: none;
    font-size: var(--rp-text-micro); font-weight: 700;
    padding: 2px 8px; border-radius: 999px;
    color: var(--st-accent, var(--text-subdued));
    background: color-mix(in srgb, var(--st-accent, var(--text-subdued)) 14%, transparent);
    border: 1px solid color-mix(in srgb, var(--st-accent, var(--text-subdued)) 45%, transparent);
    text-transform: uppercase; letter-spacing: .06em; white-space: nowrap;
  }
  .status svg { flex: none; display: block; }
  [data-rating="good"]              { --st-accent: var(--good); }
  [data-rating="needs-improvement"] { --st-accent: var(--warning); }
  [data-rating="poor"]              { --st-accent: var(--critical); }
  [data-rating="none"]              { --st-accent: var(--text-subdued); }

  /* A bordered label for a categorical tag in a dense row (device type, browser,
     HTTP method). Neutral by default — a tag is not a verdict. */
  .chip {
    display: inline-block; border: 1px solid var(--border-default); border-radius: 3px;
    padding: 0 5px; font-size: var(--rp-text-micro); font-weight: 700;
    text-transform: uppercase; letter-spacing: .06em;
    color: var(--text-subdued); background: var(--bg-surface); white-space: nowrap;
  }

  /* ── Core Web Vitals metric tile ──────────────────────────────────────────
     Ported from the portrait report, where it replaced a percentile table, and
     the reasoning carries over unchanged: LCP/INP/TTFB are milliseconds and CLS
     is a unitless shift score, so no shared numeric axis can hold them. Each
     tile normalises its metric against its OWN good/poor boundaries, which pins
     every threshold to the same x in every tile — good ends at 40%, poor begins
     at 70%. The axis is semantic rather than numeric: "how far into unacceptable
     is this" reads straight across all three, while every printed figure stays
     in its own unit. */
  .metric-grid { display: grid; gap: var(--dt-space-8); grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(200px, 1fr)); }
  .metric-tile {
    background: var(--bg-surface);
    border: 1px solid var(--border-default);
    border-radius: var(--dt-radius);
    padding: 12px 14px;
  }
  /* One accent per tile, set from the rating and consumed by the value, the
     status chip and the p75 marker, so the three can never disagree. */
  .metric-tile[data-rating="good"]              { --mt-accent: var(--good); }
  .metric-tile[data-rating="needs-improvement"] { --mt-accent: var(--warning); }
  .metric-tile[data-rating="poor"]              { --mt-accent: var(--critical); }
  .metric-tile[data-rating="none"]              { --mt-accent: var(--text-subdued); }
  .mt-head { display: flex; align-items: baseline; justify-content: space-between; gap: var(--dt-space-8); }
  .mt-abbr { font-weight: 700; font-size: var(--rp-text-title); letter-spacing: .02em; }
  .mt-name { color: var(--text-subdued); font-size: var(--rp-text-small); margin-top: 2px; }
  .mt-value { margin-top: 11px; font-weight: 700; font-size: 26px; line-height: 1; color: var(--mt-accent); }
  .mt-value-tag { font-size: var(--rp-text-small); font-weight: 600; color: var(--text-subdued); margin-left: 7px; letter-spacing: .04em; }
  /* The track carries the three threshold zones; markers sit in the same
     positioning context but outside the track's overflow, so a marker at 0% or
     100% is not clipped in half. */
  .mt-scale { position: relative; margin-top: 12px; height: 20px; }
  .mt-track { display: flex; height: 8px; border-radius: 4px; overflow: hidden; }
  /* A 2px gap of the tile surface separates adjacent fills, so the boundary
     between two zones is a real edge rather than two mixed colours meeting.
     border-box keeps the percentage widths honest once the border is added. */
  .mt-zone { height: 100%; box-sizing: border-box; }
  .mt-zone + .mt-zone { border-left: 2px solid var(--bg-surface); }
  /* Zone fills stay quieter than the markers — they are a reference backdrop,
     not data — but each is mixed only part-way toward the page base so the three
     remain tellable apart at 8px tall. */
  .mt-zone-good { width: 40%; background: color-mix(in srgb, var(--good) 45%, var(--dt-bg-base)); }
  .mt-zone-ni   { width: 30%; background: color-mix(in srgb, var(--warning) 45%, var(--dt-bg-base)); }
  .mt-zone-poor { width: 30%; background: color-mix(in srgb, var(--critical) 45%, var(--dt-bg-base)); }
  /* 2px surface ring, so a marker landing on a zone boundary or overlapping a
     neighbour stays legible as its own mark. */
  .mt-mark {
    position: absolute; top: 0; width: 2px; height: 8px; border-radius: 1px;
    background: var(--text-subdued); transform: translateX(-1px);
    box-shadow: 0 0 0 2px var(--bg-surface);
  }
  .mt-mark-p75 { width: 4px; height: 16px; top: -4px; transform: translateX(-2px); background: var(--mt-accent); }
  .mt-bounds { display: flex; justify-content: space-between; gap: var(--dt-space-8); margin-top: 2px; font-size: var(--rp-text-micro); color: var(--text-subdued); }
  .mt-tail {
    display: flex; justify-content: space-between; gap: var(--dt-space-8);
    margin-top: 9px; padding-top: 8px; border-top: 1px solid var(--border-default);
    font-size: var(--rp-text-small); color: var(--text-subdued);
  }
  .mt-tail b { color: var(--text-default); font-weight: 600; }

  /* ── Table ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
     One table idiom for every tabular panel in the deck, and deliberately the
     same row language as the portrait report: a hairline per row rather than
     zebra, uppercase micro column heads over a full-weight rule, tabular
     numerals on every figure, numeric columns shrunk to their content so the
     slack lands in the label column and the figures stay one scannable block. */
  .rp-table { width: 100%; border-collapse: collapse; font-size: var(--rp-text-small); }
  .rp-table th {
    text-align: left; padding: 0 8px 6px; white-space: nowrap;
    font-size: var(--rp-text-micro); font-weight: 700; text-transform: uppercase; letter-spacing: .06em;
    color: var(--text-subdued); border-bottom: 1px solid var(--border-default);
  }
  .rp-table td { padding: 5px 8px; border-bottom: 1px solid var(--border-subdued); vertical-align: middle; }
  .rp-table tbody tr:last-child td { border-bottom: none; }
  .rp-table .num { text-align: right; white-space: nowrap; width: 1%; }
  .rp-table .lead { font-size: var(--rp-text-body); font-weight: 600; }
  .rp-table .path { font-family: var(--dt-font-mono); color: var(--text-default); }
  /* The em dash is the mandated null mark and the one glyph that must stay
     legible, so it takes the subdued ink rather than a dimmer one: it is already
     distinct from a figure by shape and does not need to be faint as well. */
  .rp-table .nil { color: var(--text-subdued); }
  /* A row that is the current/latest period, called out so the eye can find
     "now" in a table of months without counting down it. */
  .rp-table tr.current td { color: var(--text-default); font-weight: 600; }

  /* ── Bar-in-cell ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
     The deck's signature device: it turns a numeric column you skim past into a
     bar you scan down. Each column's bar is relative to that column's own max,
     which is why the figure is always printed beside the bar — the bar shows
     shape within a column, the number carries the value. Neutral ink unless the
     cell is graded against a threshold, in which case the renderer sets
     --bar-accent from the rating. */
  .bar-cell { display: flex; align-items: center; gap: 8px; min-width: 0; }
  .bar-track { flex: 1; min-width: 44px; }
  .bar-fill { height: 18px; border-radius: 3px; min-width: 2px; background: var(--bar-accent, var(--text-subdued)); }
  .bar-value { white-space: nowrap; font-size: var(--rp-text-small); color: var(--text-default); }
  /* A track outline is the only thing that shows an empty or near-empty bar
     exists. 40% clears 3:1 against the panel; 30% did not. */
  .bar-track.outlined {
    position: relative; height: 9px; border-radius: 5px; background: var(--bg-page);
    border: 1px solid color-mix(in srgb, var(--dt-text-secondary) 40%, var(--dt-border-default));
    overflow: hidden;
  }
  .bar-track.outlined .bar-fill { height: 100%; border-radius: 0; }

  /* Flex-bar rows: the same bar-in-cell language, but as flex rows rather than a
     <table>, because a fixed-height page needs the rows to grow to fill the card
     and <tr> cannot flex. Used where the panel owns the full page height. */
  .flex-bars { display: flex; flex-direction: column; flex: 1; min-height: 0; }
  .flex-bars-header {
    display: flex; align-items: baseline; flex: 0 0 auto;
    padding: 0 10px 6px; margin-bottom: 2px; border-bottom: 1px solid var(--border-default);
  }
  .flex-bars-spacer { flex: 0 0 82px; }
  .flex-bars-col-hdr {
    flex: 1; font-size: var(--rp-text-micro); font-weight: 700;
    color: var(--text-subdued); text-transform: uppercase; letter-spacing: .06em;
  }
  .flex-bars-body { display: flex; flex-direction: column; flex: 1; min-height: 0; }
  .flex-bars-row { display: flex; align-items: center; flex: 1; min-height: 0; border-top: 1px solid var(--border-subdued); }
  .flex-bars-row:first-child { border-top: none; }
  .flex-bars-row-label { flex: 0 0 82px; padding: 0 10px; color: var(--text-subdued); font-size: var(--rp-text-small); }
  .flex-bars-cell { flex: 1; display: flex; align-items: center; padding: 5px 10px; min-width: 0; border-left: 1px solid var(--border-subdued); }
  /* The current period is marked by weight and full-strength ink, not by a
     brightness filter on the fill (v1's approach) — a filter changes a data
     mark's colour to say something about its row, which is exactly what a
     threshold hue in the same column already means. */
  .flex-bars-row.current .flex-bars-row-label,
  .flex-bars-row.current .bar-value { color: var(--text-default); font-weight: 700; }

  /* ── Chart ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
     Chart.js draws into a canvas that fills whatever the flex row gives it.
     Every chart's colours, grid ink and font come from these same tokens, read
     out of the computed style by the template's script rather than hard-coded,
     which is what lets the print override recolour the charts too. */
  .chart-body { position: relative; flex: 1; min-height: 0; min-width: 0; }
  .chart-body canvas { width: 100% !important; height: 100% !important; }
  .chart-legend { flex: 0 0 auto; display: flex; flex-wrap: wrap; gap: 4px 14px; padding-top: 8px; align-items: center; font-size: var(--rp-text-micro); color: var(--text-subdued); }
  .chart-legend .item { display: inline-flex; align-items: center; gap: 5px; }
  .chart-legend .swatch { width: 10px; height: 10px; border-radius: 2px; flex: none; }
  .chart-legend .dash { width: 14px; height: 0; border-top: 1px dashed currentColor; flex: none; }

  /* ── Findings ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
     One row per rule result: a severity chip in a fixed-width left column
     spanning both text lines, the title, then the evidence sentence. That puts
     every title on the same x, so a reader scans titles down one edge and drops
     into the evidence only where it matters. Title and evidence sit on separate
     lines because a 600-weight title running inline with a subdued sentence
     stops being scannable. */
  .finding-row {
    display: grid; grid-template-columns: 72px minmax(0, 1fr);
    gap: 3px var(--dt-space-16); padding: 9px 0;
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--border-subdued);
  }
  .finding-row:first-child { padding-top: 0; }
  .finding-row:last-child { border-bottom: none; padding-bottom: 0; }
  .finding-chip { grid-row: span 2; align-self: start; justify-self: start; margin-top: 1px; }
  .finding-title { font-weight: 600; color: var(--text-default); }
  .finding-evidence { color: var(--text-subdued); font-size: var(--rp-text-small); line-height: 1.5; overflow-wrap: anywhere; }

  /* ── Print ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
     The HTML is the review copy; the PDF is what a stakeholder gets. The screen
     canvas is dark, and browsers print text colour but not background colour by
     default — so a dark deck printed as-is would put #ebecff text on white
     paper, and forcing the dark surfaces through would flood the page with ink.
     Neither is acceptable, so print re-declares the same ten Strato roles at
     their LIGHT values (read from the same package, styles/variables.css).
     That is one block; every alias and colour-mix below :root re-derives from
     it automatically, because nothing under :root holds a raw colour.

     Chart colours are read from the computed style at build time, so the
     template's script must resolve them AFTER this override applies — see
     buildEverything()/`beforeprint` in the <script> block below. */
  @media print {
    :root {
      --dt-bg-base:             #f9f9fa; /* Colors.Background.Base.Default (light) */
      --dt-bg-container:        #f2f2f5; /* Colors.Background.Container.Neutral.Default (light) */
      --dt-bg-container-subtle: #f9f9fa; /* Colors.Background.Container.Neutral.Subdued (light) */
      --dt-text-primary:        #2f2f4f; /* Colors.Text.Neutral.Default (light) */
      --dt-text-secondary:      #595a7d; /* Colors.Text.Neutral.Subdued (light) */
      --dt-border-default:      #dadbe4; /* Colors.Border.Neutral.Default (light) */
      --dt-critical:            #bb0731; /* Colors.Text.Critical.Default (light) */
      --dt-warning:             #855400; /* Colors.Text.Warning.Default (light) */
      --dt-success:             #2d6761; /* Colors.Text.Success.Default (light) */
      --dt-primary:             #464cce; /* Colors.Text.Primary.Default (light) */
    }
    /* The page canvas is already exactly one sheet, so nothing may split and
       nothing may add a second sheet after the last one. */
    .page { break-after: page; page-break-after: always; break-inside: avoid; page-break-inside: avoid; }
    .page:last-child { break-after: auto; page-break-after: auto; }
    /* Panel surfaces are a light wash over a light page; they need
       print-color-adjust to survive, and at these values they cost almost no
       ink — the alternative is every card losing its edge on paper. */
    .panel, .kpi-tile, .metric-tile, .mini-chip,
    .bar-fill, .mt-zone, .mt-track, .status, .chip, .kpi-change {
      -webkit-print-color-adjust: exact; print-color-adjust: exact;
    }
    .notes-body[contenteditable="true"] { outline: none !important; }
  }

  /* ── Chart series palette ──────────────────────────────────────────────────
     The ten Strato roles cover surfaces, ink and the three status hues, and
     nothing else — a status hue must never be spent on a series, or "mobile"
     ends up drawn in the same amber that means "needs improvement" two panels
     away. So series get their own small categorical ramp, declared here as
     tokens for the same reason every other colour is: the print override can
     then swap the whole ramp for its light-canvas variant in one block, and the
     template's script reads the resolved values off the computed style rather
     than holding hex of its own.

     Six is the cap. Every chart in both decks tops out at four series except
     the error-type stack, which the renderer must fold into "top 5 + Other".

     Values are the light-canvas variants below (see @media print) and are
     chosen for separation at 2px line weight and at a 4mm bar, checked in
     greyscale and against deuteranopia — a stacked bar whose two segments are
     the same value in grey is a stacked bar that says nothing on a photocopy. */
  :root {
    --rp-series-1: #5eafff;  /* primary series — traffic, desktop, LCP */
    --rp-series-2: #c39bff;  /* secondary — page loads, mobile, INP */
    --rp-series-3: #6fc3ba;  /* tertiary — user actions, CLS */
    --rp-series-4: #f2a0c8;
    --rp-series-5: #f0c25a;
    --rp-series-6: #9aa4c4;  /* always the "Other" bucket — deliberately the
                                quietest, since it is a residue, not a category */
    /* Grid lines and axis ink are surface furniture, not data, and must sit
       under every series without competing. */
    --rp-grid:      color-mix(in srgb, var(--dt-border-default) 70%, transparent);
    --rp-axis-text: var(--text-subdued);
    /* Threshold reference lines on a CWV chart: the same status hues as the
       tiles, dashed, drawn under the series. Colour agrees with the tiles by
       construction because it is the same token. */
    --rp-threshold-good: color-mix(in srgb, var(--good) 70%, transparent);
    --rp-threshold-poor: color-mix(in srgb, var(--critical) 70%, transparent);
  }
  @media print {
    :root {
      --rp-series-1: #1a72c4;
      --rp-series-2: #6f3bbf;
      --rp-series-3: #2d6761;
      --rp-series-4: #b03b78;
      --rp-series-5: #8a6200;
      --rp-series-6: #6b7392;
    }
  }

  /* ── Reading a token from JS ───────────────────────────────────────────────
     Any token above whose value is a colour-mix() CANNOT be handed straight to
     Chart.js. getComputedStyle().getPropertyValue() on a custom property returns
     its *declared* text, so `--rp-grid` comes back as the literal string
     "color-mix(in srgb, #3b3b52 70%, transparent)" — and canvas strokeStyle
     silently ignores a value it cannot parse, keeping whatever colour was set
     before it. That is what painted every chart gridline near-black.

     So resolve through a probe element instead, which forces the browser to
     compute the mix down to an rgb() before JS ever sees it:

       const probe = document.createElement("span");
       probe.style.display = "none";
       document.body.appendChild(probe);
       const cssVar = (name) => {
         probe.style.color = `var(${name})`;
         return getComputedStyle(probe).color;
       };

     Use this for every token a chart consumes, not just the mixed ones — a plain
     token resolves through it unchanged, and a single accessor means a token can
     later gain a colour-mix without breaking a chart that reads it. The probe
     must be re-read on `beforeprint`, since the print override changes what the
     same name resolves to. */

  /* ── Chart colours across the theme swap: why a probe is not enough ───────
     The probe above solves colour-mix() resolution. It does NOT solve *which
     theme* is resolved, and that is the harder half.

     Measured in this repo's own render path (Chrome headless --print-to-pdf, a
     two-line test page):

         beforeprint fires:                    YES
         matchMedia("print").matches inside:   false

     So at `beforeprint` the print stylesheet has NOT been applied yet. Any
     getComputedStyle() call in that handler returns the SCREEN (dark) values,
     which is precisely what a chart rebuild there bakes into a light-printed
     PDF — near-black gridlines on white paper. Re-reading later does not help
     either: there is no event that fires after print media applies but before
     the page is captured.

     The fix is to stop asking the cascade what theme it is in and hand JS both
     palettes up front. Every token a chart consumes is declared TWICE at :root,
     in the screen value and a `-print` twin, both always present in both media:

       --ct-grid / --ct-grid-print, --ct-axis-text / --ct-axis-text-print,
       --ct-series-1 / --ct-series-1-print, … and the two threshold hues.

     The accessor then picks the set, and the print set is chosen by the caller
     rather than sniffed from the environment:

       const ctVar = (name, printing) =>
         resolve(`--ct-${name}${printing ? "-print" : ""}`);   // resolve = probe

     Charts build with `printing = false` on DOMContentLoaded and are destroyed
     and rebuilt with `printing = true` inside `beforeprint`. Because the values
     come from a token that is live in both media, the handler no longer depends
     on what the cascade has got round to applying. `Chart.defaults.animation`
     must stay `false` so the rebuild completes synchronously inside the handler,
     before the capture. Rebuild on `afterprint` with `printing = false` so a
     reader who cancels the browser's print dialog is not left with a light-inked
     chart on the dark screen copy.

     The same applies to any colour JS bakes into markup it generates — an inline
     `background:` on a legend swatch or a bar fill is a snapshot of one theme and
     has to be re-emitted on the same two events. Colours that stay in CSS
     (everything in the rules above) need none of this: the cascade handles them,
     which is the argument for keeping colour in CSS wherever a chart does not
     force your hand. */

  /* ── A transparent canvas is invisible in Preview, not just uncoloured ─────
     Chrome exports every <canvas> into a PDF as a vector transparency-group
     form XObject. Poppler (pdftoppm, and every earlier render check in this
     file's history) composites that correctly, which is exactly why it never
     caught this. Apple's CoreGraphics (Preview, `sips`, Quick Look) does not:
     it leaves the group's region fully transparent (alpha 0) and paints
     transparent as white. Measured on this deck's own output: 8.74% of a
     chart-heavy page transparent under CoreGraphics, 0% under poppler for the
     identical PDF. The holes are placed by the transparency group's bounding
     box, not by which element visually overlaps it, so they can land over a
     neighbouring masthead corner as easily as over the chart itself.

     The fix is an opaque backing store, forced before Chart.js ever touches
     the canvas:

       document.querySelectorAll("canvas").forEach(c => c.getContext("2d", { alpha: false }));

     Chart.js's own later `getContext("2d")` call returns this same cached
     context — a canvas's rendering context is fixed for its lifetime — so this
     one pass at script load covers every chart, including ones rebuilt later.

     An opaque context defaults to BLACK, not transparent, so something has to
     paint the panel colour under the data on every single frame. That has to
     be a Chart.js plugin's `beforeDraw`, not a one-off paint after the initial
     build: Chart.js's own resize/redraw passes repaint the canvas and would
     just paint back over a one-off fill. `source-over` (the default composite
     operation), not `destination-over` — there is no transparent area left for
     destination-over to land in on an opaque backing, and painting with it
     measured out to a fully black canvas. And the fill colour has to come from
     the same printing-flag system as every other chart-facing token, not from
     getComputedStyle() at draw time: reading the panel background inside
     `beforeprint` hits the exact theme-timing trap --ct-grid already exists to
     avoid, and would paint a dark panel fill onto light print paper. */
  :root {
    /* Literal hex, not var() onto --dt-* — a var() chain here would still flip
       under @media print exactly like --rp-grid does, which is the one thing
       these tokens must NOT do. Values are the same screen/print pairs as the
       --rp-* tokens above and in @media print; duplicated as fixed literals so
       both halves are live in both media regardless of which stylesheet the
       cascade has got round to applying. */
    --ct-grid:             color-mix(in srgb, #3b3b52 70%, transparent);
    --ct-grid-print:       color-mix(in srgb, #dadbe4 70%, transparent);
    --ct-axis-text:        #b1b2d2;
    --ct-axis-text-print:  #595a7d;
    --ct-series-1:         #5eafff; --ct-series-1-print: #1a72c4;
    --ct-series-2:         #c39bff; --ct-series-2-print: #6f3bbf;
    --ct-series-3:         #6fc3ba; --ct-series-3-print: #2d6761;
    --ct-series-4:         #f2a0c8; --ct-series-4-print: #b03b78;
    --ct-series-5:         #f0c25a; --ct-series-5-print: #8a6200;
    --ct-series-6:         #9aa4c4; --ct-series-6-print: #6b7392;
    --ct-critical:         #ff999c; --ct-critical-print: #bb0731;
    --ct-good:             #6fc3ba; --ct-good-print:     #2d6761;
    --ct-threshold-good:       color-mix(in srgb, #6fc3ba 70%, transparent);
    --ct-threshold-good-print: color-mix(in srgb, #2d6761 70%, transparent);
    --ct-threshold-poor:       color-mix(in srgb, #ff999c 70%, transparent);
    --ct-threshold-poor-print: color-mix(in srgb, #bb0731 70%, transparent);
    /* Panel background — see the "transparent canvas" comment above for why
       a chart canvas needs this at all (an opaque 2D context defaults to
       black and has to be painted every frame). */
    --ct-panel-bg:       #212135; /* Colors.Background.Container.Neutral.Default (dark) */
    --ct-panel-bg-print: #f2f2f5; /* Colors.Background.Container.Neutral.Default (light) */
  }

  /* ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
     Report-specific additions — everything below is specific to the
     current-month layout and is not part of the shared deck-base.css block
     above. Every colour here is still a var() onto the ten Strato roles or
     the series palette; nothing raw.
     ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ */

  /* A chart canvas is measured by Chart.js against its immediate parent at
     the moment `new Chart()` runs; deck-base.css already gives `.chart-body`
     `min-width: 0` (so a flex item can shrink below its content's natural
     size instead of forcing the row wider) but no upper clip. A canvas that
     is ever measured or resized wider than that box — a stale
     ResizeObserver tick, a rebuild racing a reflow — has nowhere to go but
     visibly past the panel, since neither `.chart-body` nor `.panel` clips
     its own content. `.page` already treats "content that doesn't fit" as a
     build-time bug rather than something to silently grow around; the same
     contract belongs one level down, at the one element a chart actually
     draws into. */
  .chart-body { overflow: hidden; }

  /* Page 1: four analyst-note panels read as quadrants, not a list — a CSS
     grid (not the flex two-column-of-two v1 used) gives all four an equal
     share of the page's remaining height regardless of which one's prose
     happens to run longer, so a short "Errors" note doesn't leave its card
     visibly emptier than "Traffic" next to it. */
  .findings-grid {
    display: grid; grid-template-columns: repeat(2, 1fr); grid-template-rows: repeat(2, 1fr);
    gap: var(--dt-space-16); flex: 1; min-height: 0;
  }
  .findings-grid .panel { min-height: 0; }

  /* Page 3: three metrics side by side under one panel roof (distribution
     histograms, daily trends). A bare chart-body has no way to say which of
     the three columns is LCP vs. INP vs. CLS other than the axis units, so
     each gets a micro label — the same eyebrow language as .panel-title, one
     level down. */
  .chart-col { flex: 1; min-width: 0; display: flex; flex-direction: column; min-height: 0; }
  .chart-col-title {
    flex: 0 0 auto; margin: 0 0 4px; text-align: center;
    font-size: var(--rp-text-micro); font-weight: 700;
    text-transform: uppercase; letter-spacing: .06em; color: var(--text-subdued);
  }

  /* The Experience Summary row was taking roughly twice the height of either
     chart row below it (three full metric-tiles, each with its own head,
     name line, value, scale, bounds and tail), which left the daily-trend
     line charts under 60px of plot height — flat, indistinguishable ribbons
     with unreadable threshold dashes. This page's tiles only need to repeat
     what a reader can already see on page 1's KPI row plus the tier split;
     `.compact` tightens the internal spacing so the row reads as a strip of
     reference figures rather than a fourth chart competing for the same
     space as the two rows that actually carry shape (distribution across the
     month, change day over day) — the whole reason page 3 exists. */
  .metric-grid.compact .metric-tile { padding: 8px 12px; }
  .metric-grid.compact .mt-name { display: none; }
  .metric-grid.compact .mt-value { margin-top: 5px; font-size: 21px; }
  .metric-grid.compact .mt-scale { margin-top: 7px; height: 16px; }
  .metric-grid.compact .mt-bounds { margin-top: 1px; }
  .metric-grid.compact .mt-tail { margin-top: 6px; padding-top: 6px; }

  /* A plain-text threshold grade for a table cell: the same --st-accent a
     .status chip or .metric-tile consumes, but spent on the figure itself
     rather than a pill, because a scorecard row with six graded columns
     already has an accent-coloured .bar-fill in the same row — a chip per
     cell on top of that reads as six separate alerts instead of one row's
     worth of context. Weight carries the same "this is a verdict, not just a
     number" signal a chip's border would, at a fraction of the width. */
  .grade[data-rating] { color: var(--st-accent); font-weight: 600; }

  /* A bar-in-cell column needs real width to show proportion between rows;
     deck-base's `.num` (right-align + shrink-to-content) is right for a plain
     figure but wrong for a bar, so bar columns carry both classes and this
     rule un-shrinks them. See the renderTable() comment for what went wrong
     without it. */
  .rp-table .bar-col { width: auto; }

  /* An exception message is open-ended analyst/stack-trace text with no
     natural length limit — sized-for-content would give this table a
     different height than Top Request Errors beside it on every real
     dataset. One line, clipped, keeps both tables the same height instead.

     `white-space: nowrap` alone tells the browser's table auto-layout that
     this cell wants to be as wide as its full, untruncated text — `overflow`
     and `text-overflow` only clip what gets *painted*, not what the layout
     algorithm reserves space for, so the table itself grew wider than its
     panel and the real overflow surfaced three columns over, as Source/Path
     text silently cut off (no ellipsis) at the page edge. table-layout:fixed
     switches these two tables to CSS-specified column widths — the narrow
     columns get an explicit width below, and Message/Path (unspecified)
     split whatever's left, so cropping only ever happens in the one column
     it's meant to. */
  #table-topExceptions, #table-topRequestErrors { table-layout: fixed; }
  /* Column 1 holds a bar-cell (bar-track min-width 44px + gap + a 2-3 digit
     value), not a bare number — a width tighter than that doesn't shrink the
     bar, it just lets the bar-cell's own content paint past the cell
     boundary and collide with the next column's text. */
  #table-topExceptions th:nth-child(1), #table-topExceptions td:nth-child(1),
  #table-topRequestErrors th:nth-child(1), #table-topRequestErrors td:nth-child(1) { width: 86px; }
  #table-topExceptions th:nth-child(2), #table-topExceptions td:nth-child(2) { width: 104px; }
  #table-topExceptions th:nth-child(4), #table-topExceptions td:nth-child(4) { width: 140px; }
  #table-topRequestErrors th:nth-child(2), #table-topRequestErrors td:nth-child(2) { width: 66px; }
  #table-topRequestErrors th:nth-child(3), #table-topRequestErrors td:nth-child(3) { width: 56px; }
  .rp-table td.msg {
    white-space: nowrap; overflow: hidden; text-overflow: ellipsis;
  }
  /* `.path` is a <span>, and `overflow`/`text-overflow` on an inline element
     with no width of its own is a no-op — that's why Source/Path text kept
     bleeding past the card edge even with `white-space: nowrap` (which DOES
     apply to inline text) already set below: nowrap stopped it wrapping, but
     nothing was clipping the now-single-line overflow. `display: block`
     gives it the cell's own width to overflow *against*, so the same
     ellipsis rule the Message column gets from being a real table cell
     works here too. */
  .rp-table .path { display: block; white-space: nowrap; overflow: hidden; text-overflow: ellipsis; }

  /* Page 4: Top Pages is a single panel that owns the whole page height, and
     an <rp-table> sized to its own 10 rows left ~200px of dead space below
     the last row on a fixed-height page — reading as an unfinished page
     rather than a complete scorecard. `.flex-bars` (already in the shared
     layer, built for exactly this: a fixed-height page whose <tr> can't
     flex) replaces the table so the 10 rows grow to fill the panel instead.
     The shared 82px row-label width assumes a short label (a month, a
     device); a page path like "/product/[id]" needs more, hence
     `.wide-label`. */
  .flex-bars.wide-label .flex-bars-spacer,
  .flex-bars.wide-label .flex-bars-row-label { flex: 0 0 170px; }
  .flex-bars-cell.num { justify-content: flex-end; }
  .flex-bars-col-hdr.num { text-align: right; }

  /* Inline code inside an analyst's note (a literal page path, exception
     name, or figure the prose calls out) — the same mono face + subdued
     surface the rest of the deck uses for a literal value (.rp-table .path),
     just small enough to sit inline in a sentence. */
  .notes-body code {
    font-family: var(--dt-font-mono); font-size: .93em;
    background: var(--bg-page); border: 1px solid var(--border-subdued);
    border-radius: 3px; padding: 0 4px;
  }

  /* Findings panel: the same idiom as the finding-row list defined above, but
     the current-month deck has no severity-ranked rule engine — its notes are
     freeform analyst prose (see .notes-body) — so .finding-row itself is
     unused here and is only present because it travelled in with the pasted
     shared layer. Left in rather than stripped: the trending deck does use it,
     and a future current-month finding source (e.g. an automated rule pass)
     can adopt it without another round of copying CSS between the two decks. */
</style>
</head>
